    "befool": {
      "word": "BEFOOL",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To fool; to delude or lead into error; to infatuate; to deceive. This story . . . contrived to befool credulous men. Fuller.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cause to behave like a fool; to make foolish. \"Some befooling drug.\" G. Eliot.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "before": {
      "word": "BEFORE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "In front of; preceding in space; ahead of; as, to stand before the fire; before the house. His angel, who shall go Before them in a cloud and pillar of fire. Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Preceding in time; earlier than; previously to; anterior to the time when; -- sometimes with the additional idea of purpose; in order that. Before Abraham was, I am. John viii. 58. Before this treatise can become of use, two points are necessary. Swift. that Philip called thee . . . I saw thee.\" John i. 48.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "An advance of; farther onward, in place or time. The golden age . . . is before us. Carlyle.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Prior or preceding in dignity, order, rank, right, or worth; rather than. He that cometh after me is preferred before me. John i. 15. The eldest son is before the younger in succession. Johnson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "In presence or sight of; face to face with; facing. Abraham bowed down himself before the people. Gen. xxiii. 12. Wherewith shall I come before the Lord Micah vi. 6.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Under the cognizance or jurisdiction of. If a suit be begun before an archdeacon. Ayliffe.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Open for; free of access to; in the power of. The world was all before them where to choose. Milton. Before the mast (Naut.), as a common sailor, -- because the sailors live in the forecastle, forward of the foremast. impulse; having the wind aft.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "On the fore part; in front, or in the direction of the front; -- opposed to in the rear. The battle was before and behind. 2 Chron. xiii. 14.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "In advance. \"I come before to tell you.\" Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "In time past; previously; already. You tell me, mother, what I knew before. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Earlier; sooner than; until then. When the butt is out, we will drink water; not a drop before. Shak. cited, before-mentioned; beforesaid.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        }
      ],
